g need to deploy IPv6. - **Self-interest and inertia.** ISPs, companies, and vendors looked at the cost of upgrading all equipment, retraining staff, and potentially breaking something that already "worked" with NAT, and said: "Why bother? We'll just buy more IPv4 addresses or share them." It was cheaper and easier in the short term. - **A classic tragedy of the commons.** The benefit of staying on IPv4 (no upgrade cost) was private and immediate. The cost (exhaustion, bad connectivity for others, future internet fragility) was distributed and delayed. So almost everyone rationally chose the messy workaround. - **IPv6 arrived at the worst time.** It was finalized just as the dot-com boom/bust happened and NAT became ubiquitous. It was solving a problem (address exhaustion) that NAT had papered over for the masses. The compelling "killer app" for IPv6 just wasn't there for average businesses. So the clean, proactive plan (IPv6 everywhere) was effectively abandoned by the market in favor of the messy, reactive strategy: "Extend IPv4's life with whatever duct tape we can find." ### The "Workaround Stack" Became the New Plan What you call "messy workarounds" became the *de facto* operational plan for the last two decades: - **Phase 1:** Home NAT so ISPs don't need to give every device an address. - **Phase 2:** Carrier-Grade NAT so ISPs don't need to give every subscriber an address. - **Phase 3:** An open market to trade IPv4 blocks like pork bellies, ensuring those who can pay most (big cloud, big ISPs) will always get addresses. - **Phase 4:** For those who can't afford IPv4, run IPv6-only with translators (NAT64/DNS64) to reach the IPv4 internet, effectively treating IPv4 as a legacy service reachable through a translation layer. Nobody sat down in 1998 and said, "Right, plan A is NAT, plan B is a speculative market, and plan C is translation kludges." But that's exactly the patchwork system that evolved because the original plan was discarded by collective inaction. ### So, Was the "Real Plan" Just the Mess? In a way, yes. The internet industry's practical answer to "what's the plan for running out of IPv4?" turned out to be: "We'll let temporary NAT hacks buy us decades, combined with a market for addresses, while a minority slowly migrates to IPv6, and then we'll just live with a permanently fractured network where everything is translated and shared." It's not an elegant, clean, forward-looking plan. It's the technological equivalent of never cleaning your apartment and just buying more shelves to pile things on, until finally you're forced to dig a tunnel to a new, empty apartment (IPv6) but you still keep going back to the old cluttered one through a hatch in the floor. Your frustration is completely justified. You were promised a well-engineered transition plan, and what you got was a bunch of brilliant but messy hacks that became the new normal. The only silver lining is that those hacks have worked surprisingly well for most people—well enough that the original plan never got the momentum it deserved.
